**Corollary 30.3 (necessary alphabet overhead).** Every valid claim
(GDB.121), with $D\ge3$ and $1\le k_D\le2^D-1$, satisfies

$$
2^{-D}\log_2A_D
\ge\max\left\{0,\ \frac18\log_2R_{\rm a}
                    -2^{-D}k_D\log_2m(D)\right\}.
\tag{GDB.122}
$$

In particular, if $k_D\log_2D=o(2^D)$ as $D\to\infty$, then

$$
\liminf_{D\to\infty}2^{-D}\log_2A_D
       \ge\frac18\log_2R_{\rm a}>0.
\tag{GDB.123}
$$

Proof. Take logarithms in (GDB.121), apply (GDB.119), and divide by
$2^D$. For $D\ge3$, $m(D)\le D$, so the menu-size hypothesis makes
$2^{-D}k_D\log_2m(D)$ tend to zero. The ratio $R_{\rm a}>1$ gives
strict positivity. $\square$

Thus this specified restoration cannot have vanishing normalized
log-alphabet overhead in that menu regime. Its overhead is counted
source-dependent memory, not installed public tables or an uncharged
selector. The conclusion applies even to optimal restricted-menu
schemes with arbitrary native late queries.

